item.description:
arXiv:2611.31484v1 Announce Type: new 
Abstract: We study Brownian first passage to a finite polynomial boundary \[
  F(t)=d+\sum_{j=2}^p c_jt^j. \] For every such boundary we construct a time-independent signed exterior image measure. After Brownian scaling its density is determined by a second-kind Volterra equation whose kernel is the multivariate Wright series \[
  \mathcal K_{\boldsymbol\beta}(u,v)
  =\sum_{\bn\ne\mathbf0}
  \frac{\boldsymbol\beta^{\bn}v^{|\bn|/2}
  (u-v)^{\kappa(\bn)-1}}
  {2^{\kappa(\bn)}\bn!\Gamma(\kappa(\bn))},
  \qquad
  \kappa(\bn)=\sum_{j=2}^p(j-1)\nu_j. \] An exact multivariate finite-difference cancellation yields exponential tail control of the image density. Consequently, on a nonzero interval of the natural Brownian scale, its heat potential is the killed transition kernel and its boundary flux is the first-passage density.
  The image kernel is connected exactly with the differential transported-zero theory. A two-variable beta-moment transform carries its multivariate Wright seed to the entire constant-flux function associated with the polynomial boundary. Writing $K(\bn)=\sum j\nu_j$ and $W(\bn)=\sum(2j-1)\nu_j$, we construct the graded spatial transmutation and prove the Laplace conjugacy \[
  \Lap\A_{\bn}\Lap^{-1}
  =\frac{(-1)^{|\bn|}}{\bn!}k^{|\bn|}
  \left(-\frac1k\frac{d}{dk}\right)^{K(\bn)}. \] Thus the inverse transmutation generates the formal parameter expansion of the same first-passage problem. For $F(t)=At^2+Bt^3$ we also derive fifth- and eighth-order scalar annihilators and prove their exact rational factorization. Numerical experiments for two- and three-term polynomial boundaries independently agree with Durbin's equation to discretization accuracy.
